  "answers": {
    "netmedex_hybrid_rag": "**Direct mechanistic evidence (animal)**  \n- Intestinal‑epithelial‑specific Cftr knockout mice develop a reproducible shift in gut composition (↑Proteobacteria, Clostridia, Enterobacteriaceae, loss of Akkermansia) together with elevated fecal calprotectin, indicating that loss of CFTR in the epithelium is sufficient to generate dysbiosis *and* mucosal inflammation【PMID:37546931】【PMID:38073491】.  \n- Whole‑body Cftr‑KO mice display the same dysbiotic signature and increased intestinal permeability; restoring CFTR only in the gut (CFTR‑KO‑GC) normalises the microbiota and abolishes peri‑portal liver inflammation, showing that dysbiosis downstream of CFTR loss drives inflammatory pathology【PMID:38554847】.\n\n**Human association supporting each hop**  \n- A systematic review of 38 studies confirms that people with CF harbor reduced microbial diversity and enrichment of pro‑inflammatory taxa (Enterobacteriaceae, Proteobacteria) compared with healthy controls, linking CFTR dysfunction to dysbiosis【PMID:36600119】.  \n- In a longitudinal cohort, ivacaftor‑mediated CFTR potentiation increased Akkermansia, decreased Enterobacteriaceae, and concurrently lowered stool calprotectin, demonstrating that improving CFTR function ameliorates dysbiosis and intestinal inflammation in humans【PMID:30546102】.\n\n**Inference that dysbiosis mediates inflammation**  \n- The gut‑corrected mouse model (CFTR‑KO‑GC) uncouples CFTR loss from dysbiosis; absence of dysbiosis prevents the inflammatory cascade, providing a testable hypothesis that dysbiotic microbes are necessary effectors of inflammation downstream of CFTR deficiency【PMID:38554847】.  \n- Non‑absorbable antibiotic treatment in CFTR‑KO mice reduces both dysbiosis and liver inflammation, further supporting a causal role for the altered microbiota in mediating inflammatory responses【PMID:38554847】.\n\n**Overall mechanistic path**  \nCFTR loss → altered ion/water transport in intestinal epithelium → selective pressure favoring Proteobacteria/Clostridia, loss of beneficial taxa → microbial dysbiosis → increased permeability and microbial‑associated molecular patterns → activation of innate immune cells → elevated calprotectin and tissue inflammation.\n\n**Conclusion**: Multiple animal experiments and human therapeutic observations collectively substantiate a CFTR‑to‑dysbiosis‑to‑inflammation axis.\n\nGraph-derived candidate insights\n- PATH 3a03360fb323a847 — GRAPH-CONFIRMED; multi-hop inference; HOP 1: cftr induces dysbiosis [PMID: 37546931]; HOP 2: dysbiosis associated_with inflammation [PMID: 38554847].\n- PATH daf58598d61d93bf — GRAPH-CONFIRMED; direct graph evidence; HOP 1: cftr induces inflammation [PMID: 37546931]."
  },
